---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 17</p>
  <h1>Clothes &amp; <em>shopping</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Build the whole shopping message, not only a list of nouns. Learn why <span class="ex">these jeans are</span> but <span class="ex">this pair of jeans is</span>, how <span class="ex">it</span> changes to <span class="ex">them</span>, and how to ask about price, size, and fit before you choose to buy or politely decline.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">clothes · clothing · pair</span>
    <span class="tag">this / that / these / those · it / them</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare one item, two items, and one plural-form garment.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Follow the noun from demonstrative to verb and pronoun.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Choose price, size, fit, and try-on language.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Help a fictional customer choose or decline.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Clothes</span> is a plural word: <span class="ex">These clothes are clean.</span> It is not countable, so do not say <span class="ex">a clothes</span> or <span class="ex">two clothes</span>. Count a specific garment or item instead: <span class="ex">a shirt</span>, <span class="ex">two jackets</span>, or <span class="ex">three items of clothing</span>. <span class="ex">Clothing</span> is uncountable and takes a singular verb: <span class="ex">This protective clothing is new.</span></p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved near and far demonstratives plus some with uncountable clothing.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the physical scene and the noun type. Say each complete answer aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="this">this</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that">that</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="these">these</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="those">those</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="some">some</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a">a</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The customer is holding one shirt: “I like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="this" data-hint="One nearby thing takes this." data-fix="I like this shirt." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> shirt.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The boots are across the room: “How much are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="those" data-hint="More than one thing farther away takes those." data-fix="How much are those boots?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> boots?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The donation list uses the uncountable category: “We need <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="some" data-hint="Clothing is uncountable, so a cannot introduce it." data-fix="We need some warm clothing." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> warm clothing.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet the clothes and accessories</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Learn the item with its number pattern. The picture does not determine the grammar by itself.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/tshirt.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">T-shirt</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/jeans.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">jeans</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/dress.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">dress</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/coat.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">coat</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/sneakers.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">sneakers</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/shoes.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">shoes</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/socks.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">socks</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/cap.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">cap</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/scarf.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">scarf</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/gloves.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">gloves</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/handbag.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">handbag</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/shopping-bags.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">shopping bags</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/shorts.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">shorts</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/tie.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">tie</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/boots.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">boots</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/sandals.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">sandals</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/glasses.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">glasses</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/sunglasses.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">sunglasses</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/watch.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">watch</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/ring.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">ring</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/hat.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">hat</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/backpack.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">backpack</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/umbrella.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">umbrella</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/purse.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">purse</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice two different plural pictures</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>One display, three noun patterns</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">This jacket is $48. These shoes are $36. These jeans are $42, but this pair of jeans is on sale.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Can you separate the one ordinary item, two separate items, and one garment with plural grammar?</li>
      <li>Which word controls the verb in <span class="ex">this pair of jeans is</span>: <span class="ex">pair</span> or <span class="ex">jeans</span>?</li>
      <li>When a later sentence refers to the jacket, shoes, or jeans, would the pronoun be <span class="ex">it</span> or <span class="ex">them</span>?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You followed ordinary singulars, plural nouns, uncountable clothing, and the number of pair.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose is or are</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Find the grammatical head before choosing the verb.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Singular and plural verb choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is">is</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are">are</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>This jacket <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is" data-hint="Jacket is one ordinary countable item." data-fix="This jacket is $48." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> $48.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>These shoes <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="Shoes names two separate items here." data-fix="These shoes are $36." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> $36.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>These jeans <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="Jeans has plural grammar even when it means one garment." data-fix="These jeans are $42." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> $42.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>This protective clothing <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is" data-hint="Clothing is an uncountable singular category word." data-fix="This protective clothing is new." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> new.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>This pair of jeans <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is" data-hint="The head noun is singular pair; of jeans identifies the pair." data-fix="This pair of jeans is on sale." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on sale.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>These two pairs of jeans <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="The head noun is plural pairs." data-fix="These two pairs of jeans are on sale." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on sale.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: the noun controls the chain</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>English agreement forms a chain. Choose the noun picture first, then keep the demonstrative, verb, and pronoun consistent.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>noun picture</th><th>demonstrative + noun + verb</th><th>refer back</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>one item near</td><td><span class="ex">This shirt is blue.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Can I try it on?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>one item farther away</td><td><span class="ex">That coat is warm.</span></td><td><span class="ex">How much is it?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>more than one near</td><td><span class="ex">These shoes are black.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Can I try them on?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>more than one farther away</td><td><span class="ex">Those gloves are small.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Do you have them in a large?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Two kinds of plural are not the same</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Shoes, socks</span>, and <span class="ex">gloves</span> can name two separate physical items. One item can be <span class="ex">a shoe</span>, <span class="ex">a sock</span>, or <span class="ex">a glove</span>.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Pants, jeans</span>, and <span class="ex">shorts</span> normally name one garment but use plural grammar. Do not use <span class="ex">a pant</span> or <span class="ex">a jean</span> for the complete garment. Say <span class="ex">a pair of pants</span> or <span class="ex">a pair of jeans</span>.</p>
    <p>The viewpoint can change the pronoun: <span class="ex">I like these jeans. I’ll take them.</span> But <span class="ex">I like this pair. I’ll take it.</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept distance, number, and later pronoun reference consistent across each shopping scene.">
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the demonstrative or pronoun</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Demonstrative and pronoun choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="this">this</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that">that</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="these">these</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="those">those</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="it">it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="them">them</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The customer is holding one shirt: “Do you have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="this" data-hint="One item near the speaker takes this." data-fix="Do you have this shirt in blue?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> shirt in blue?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The coat is across the room: “Is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="that" data-hint="One item farther from the speaker takes that." data-fix="Is that coat on sale?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> coat on sale?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The shoes are beside the customer: “Can I try <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="these" data-hint="More than one nearby item takes these before the noun." data-fix="Can I try these shoes on?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> shoes on?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The boots are on the far display: “How much are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="those" data-hint="More than one farther item takes those." data-fix="How much are those boots?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> boots?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The clerk brings one jacket. The customer asks, “Can I try <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="it" data-hint="Refer back to one jacket with it." data-fix="Can I try it on?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The clerk brings jeans. The customer says, “I’ll try <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="them" data-hint="Jeans has plural grammar, so refer back with them." data-fix="I’ll try them on." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Four clothing actions</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>verb</th><th>core meaning</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>wear</b></td><td>the clothes are on the body; this is a state</td><td><span class="ex">The clerk is wearing a blue shirt.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>put on</b></td><td>move clothing onto the body</td><td><span class="ex">Put on the jacket before you go outside.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>take off</b></td><td>remove clothing from the body</td><td><span class="ex">Take off the coat in the fitting room.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>try on</b></td><td>test clothing before deciding</td><td><span class="ex">Can I try these shoes on?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Try on</span> is separable. A full noun can go after or between the two parts: <span class="ex">try on the jacket</span> or <span class="ex">try the jacket on</span>. A short pronoun must go in the middle: <span class="ex">try it on</span>, <span class="ex">try them on</span>, not <span class="ex">try on it</span> or <span class="ex">try on them</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the clothing state from dressing, removing, and testing actions.">
    <h3>Drill 2: choose the action from the situation</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Clothing action choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wear">wear</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="put on">put on</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="take off">take off</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="try on">try on</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The profile describes the clerk’s usual work clothes: “They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wear" data-hint="Use wear for the state or usual clothing choice." data-fix="They wear a blue shirt at work." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a blue shirt at work.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The customer wants the jacket on their body now: “Please <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="put on" data-hint="Use put on for the action of moving clothing onto the body." data-fix="Please put on the jacket." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the jacket.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The customer is ready to remove the jacket: “You can <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="take off" data-hint="Use take off for removing clothing." data-fix="You can take off the jacket now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the jacket now.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The customer wants to test the size before deciding: “Can I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="try on" data-hint="Use try on when testing clothing before a decision." data-fix="Can I try on the jacket?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the jacket?”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Move through a shopping conversation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>one item</th><th>plural item or pair</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>find an item</td><td><span class="ex">I’m looking for a jacket.</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’m looking for black shoes.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ask about color or size</td><td><span class="ex">Do you have this in a medium?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Do you have these in size 9?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>test it</td><td><span class="ex">Can I try it on?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Can I try them on?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>report fit</td><td><span class="ex">It fits. It’s too tight.</span></td><td><span class="ex">They fit. They’re too big.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ask price</td><td><span class="ex">How much is it?</span></td><td><span class="ex">How much are they?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>choose</td><td><span class="ex">I’ll take it.</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’ll take them.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>decline politely</td><td><span class="ex">I’ll think about it, thanks.</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’ll think about them, thanks.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Size, fit, and sale do different jobs</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Size</span> is a label or measurement. A salesperson may ask <span class="ex">What size do you need?</span> A customer can ask <span class="ex">Do you have this in a medium?</span> or <span class="ex">Do you have these in size 9?</span> Clothing and shoe size systems vary, so the number alone is not universal.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Fit</span> describes whether the physical size and shape work: <span class="ex">It fits</span>, <span class="ex">They fit</span>, <span class="ex">It’s too tight</span>, <span class="ex">They’re too loose</span>, <span class="ex">too short</span>, or <span class="ex">too long</span>.</p>
    <p>In common U.S. shopping English, <span class="ex">on sale</span> usually means the price is reduced. <span class="ex">For sale</span> means available to buy. An item can be for sale without having a lower price.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">I’ll take it</span> means “I choose it and will buy it.” A customer can also leave without buying: <span class="ex">It doesn’t fit, but thank you</span> or <span class="ex">I’ll think about it, thanks.</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>U.S. clothing words</h3>
    <p>In U.S. English, <span class="ex">pants</span> is the ordinary word for the outer garment; British English commonly uses <span class="ex">trousers</span>. British <span class="ex">pants</span> can mean underwear, so context matters. U.S. <span class="ex">sweater</span> often corresponds to British <span class="ex">jumper</span>.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Sneakers</span> is a broad U.S. word for casual athletic shoes. Some U.S. speakers also use <span class="ex">tennis shoes</span> broadly. <span class="ex">Purse</span> is common in U.S. English for a bag carried for personal items; <span class="ex">handbag</span> is also used.</p>
    <p>A U.S. price such as <span class="ex">$24.99</span> may be read casually as <span class="ex">twenty-four ninety-nine</span> or fully as <span class="ex">twenty-four dollars and ninety-nine cents</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: hear the number and the message</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Clothes</span> is normally one syllable. Do not add a separate vowel before the final sounds. <span class="ex">Clothing</span> has two syllables: <span class="ex"><strong>KLOH</strong>-thing</span>.</p>
    <p>Make the voiced <span class="ex">th</span> and vowel difference audible in <span class="ex">this</span>, <span class="ex">these</span>, <span class="ex">that</span>, and <span class="ex">those</span>. Keep plural endings clear: <span class="ex">shirts</span> ends /s/, <span class="ex">shoes</span> ends /z/, and <span class="ex">sizes</span> adds a final syllable /ɪz/.</p>
    <p>In connected speech, the small words link: <span class="ex">Can-I try-it-<strong>ON</strong>?</span> and <span class="ex">Do-you-<strong>HAVE</strong> this in a medium?</span> <span class="ex">I’ll</span> is one syllable in <span class="ex">I’ll <strong>TAKE</strong> it</span>. These are listening guides, not new spellings.</p>
  </div>

