  <h2>Use it safely and flexibly</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real clothes, body, size, purchases, budget, brands, work, religion, health, mobility, travel, home, family, gender, or appearance. Use the fictional store, catalog, customer cards, and invented details, or share personal information only when you freely choose.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Sort the fictional display:</b> Make three groups: ordinary singular items, two separate-item plurals, and one-garment plural words. Add one correct demonstrative, verb, and pronoun chain to each group.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Move through the store:</b> Ask about one nearby item, one farther item, one nearby plural item, and one farther plural item. Keep <span class="ex">this / that / these / those</span> and <span class="ex">is / are</span> consistent.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Handle size and fit:</b> The teacher supplies fictional size cards. Ask for another size, ask to try the item on, and report <span class="ex">fits</span>, <span class="ex">too tight</span>, <span class="ex">too loose</span>, <span class="ex">too short</span>, or <span class="ex">too long</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Protect customer choice:</b> Practice both decisions: <span class="ex">I’ll take it / them</span> and <span class="ex">I’ll think about it / them, thanks</span>. No explanation for declining is required.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: help a fictional customer compare two items for 45–60 seconds.</b> Identify one singular and one plural-form item, state size or color, ask to try <span class="ex">it</span> or <span class="ex">them</span> on, report fit, ask the price, explain whether <span class="ex">on sale</span> means discounted, and finish by buying or politely declining.</div>

